<figure class="device-study" aria-labelledby="device-study-title">
  <figcaption id="device-study-title">
    <span>INTERFACE MAP / LINUX DRIVER</span>
    <strong>Same value, different layer owns the conversion</strong>
  </figcaption>
  <p class="input">Starting point: host-order <code>u32 value = 0x12345678</code>.</p>
  <div class="paths">
    <div class="path">
      <strong>01 · Standard LE MMIO register</strong>
      <p class="step"><code>writel(value, reg)</code></p>
      <p>The accessor takes a host value and implements the register’s little-endian contract. Don’t pass <code>cpu_to_le32(value)</code> to it.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="path">
      <strong>02 · LE DMA descriptor field in RAM</strong>
      <p class="step"><code>d-&gt;length = cpu_to_le32(value)</code></p>
      <p>Here the driver encodes the field before storing it as <code>__le32</code>. The bytes at increasing RAM addresses are <code>78 56 34 12</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="separate"><strong>Separate question:</strong> When may the device consume the descriptor? Byte conversion does not establish visibility, ownership, or doorbell ordering.</p>
</figure>

